@*
    ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    BnSafeAreaDemo — Phase 14.2 Task 6 (#338), route "/safearea", mount-registry
    key "BnSafeAreaDemo". The cross-shell parity proof for BnSafeArea, on the
    same footing as BnLayoutDemo/BnScrollDemo/BnImageDemo: its frame table
    (bnSafeAreaDemoFrames) is asserted identically on the AVD and the iOS
    simulator by ShellFrameTableDriftTests.

    UNLIKE those three pages, real device insets differ between an AVD and a
    simulator (and between devices), so a table built from WHATEVER the device
    reports could never agree number-for-number. The device tests dispatch
    "safeAreaChanged" themselves, with the SAME fixed values on both shells,
    before reading this page's laid-out frame — exercising the real wire path
    (MainActivity's WindowInsets listener / HostViewController's layout hook →
    DispatchHostSafeArea → BnSafeAreaInsets.Current → this component) rather
    than a test-only override seam.

    ── WHY THE CONTENT BOX PROVES ALL FOUR EDGES, NOT JUST TWO ───────────────
    A naive "one fixed box, read its x/y" proves TopEdge and LeftEdge (the
    box's own origin) but says nothing about RightEdge/BottomEdge — nothing
    in that shape reacts to them. This page instead gives BnSafeArea an
    EXPLICIT Width/Height (300×200, so the assertion never depends on the
    test device's actual screen size) and fills it with a SINGLE Grow="1"
    child: Yoga's default column stretch sizes that child to the padding
    box's full extent, i.e. width = 300 − left − right and
    height = 200 − top − bottom. So one child's frame —
    (left, top, 300−left−right, 200−top−bottom) — is a function of all four
    edges at once, and a wrong edge (an off-by-one in TryParseEdge, an
    "additive" that silently replaced instead of summed, a transposed
    left/right) moves a number this table would catch.

    "← Back" sits OUTSIDE the BnSafeArea (a second root-level node) rather
    than sharing its fixed 200dp height with a Grow="1" box that already
    claims all of it — this page is a geometry proof, not a capability demo,
    and the nav control does not need to be inset-aware to be tappable, since
    it never has to compete with system UI on the pages that actually house
    real content (the four capability pages already wrap it correctly).
    ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*@
<BnSafeArea Width="300" Height="200">
    <BnView Grow="1" BackgroundColor="#F44336" />
</BnSafeArea>
<BnButton Label="← Back" OnClick="GoBack" />

@code {
    [Inject] public INavigationManager Navigation { get; set; } = default!;

    // Sync-completing (inline dispatcher), like every other page's GoBack.
    private Task GoBack() => Navigation.NavigateToAsync("/").AsTask();
}
